language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ session2_subsets_100: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Out of a set A of different integers, find two disjoint non-empty subsets S and T of A whose elements have equal sums. S union T need not be all of A.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `session2_subsets_100.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/session2_subsets_100.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `session2_subsets_100_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py session2_subsets_100.json
$   savilerow model.eprime session2_subsets_100_1.param -run-solver

$ A_dim1: the number of elements of A (derived by the converter from the data; not a field of the reader's JSON)
given A_dim1 : int
$ the elements of A are numbered 1 to n
letting n = A_dim1
$ A[i]: the i-th element of the set A (indices 1-based)
given A : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int

$ in_S[i]: element i of A is in the subset S
find in_S : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of bool
$ in_T[i]: element i of A is in the subset T
find in_T : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of bool

$ search hint: branch on the two subsets (speeds up the search)
branching on [in_S, in_T]

such that
$ S is non-empty
exists i : int(1..n) . in_S[i],
$ T is non-empty
exists i : int(1..n) . in_T[i],
$ S and T are disjoint: no element of A is in both
forAll i : int(1..n) . !(in_S[i] /\ in_T[i]),
$ the elements of S and T have the same sum
(sum i : int(1..n) . toInt(in_S[i]) * A[i]) = (sum i : int(1..n) . toInt(in_T[i]) * A[i])
